# shellcheck configuration — see tools/lint.sh for the run itself.
#
# Every entry here suppresses evidence, so every entry carries the reason it is
# not actionable. A code that fires on something a contributor could actually
# fix does not belong in this file; it belongs in the findings.

# config.sh is a sourced configuration fragment, so it correctly has no shebang
# and shellcheck cannot infer the dialect. Measured on a fragment with no
# shebang: without this line shellcheck adds "SC2148 (error): target shell is
# unknown" on top of the file's real findings; with it, only the real findings
# remain. The file is analysed either way — this removes an un-actionable ERROR
# (config.sh must not gain a shebang), it does not unlock the analysis.
# Files that DO carry a shebang are unaffected: this is the fallback dialect.
shell=bash

# SC1090 — "can't follow non-constant source". claude-worktime.sh:243 sources
# the user's config from an XDG path resolved at runtime; the path is
# non-constant by design and no directive can make it static. The finding is
# therefore permanently un-actionable rather than merely unfixed.
disable=SC1090

# Deliberately NOT disabled, though it is the largest class: SC2034 "appears
# unused". Most of it is the GROUP_* statusline defaults, which are a documented
# user-override surface reached by indirection (${!var_name}) and only look
# unused; the rest (ts_cc, ts_cr, ts_out, ts_ui, st_t, HOME_ORG,
# PROJECT_GIT_ANCHOR) have not been shown to be false positives. Blanketing the
# code would hide those with the harmless ones, so they stay visible in
# docs/lint-baseline-2026-08-08.txt.
#
# The split is measured, never remembered — a count written here goes stale the
# next time a group is added, silently, because nothing checks it:
#   tools/lint.sh --format=gcc | grep -c SC2034             # all of them
#   tools/lint.sh --format=gcc | grep SC2034 | grep -c GROUP_
# 29 and 20 as of 2026-08-14, replacing a stale 26/18 that also named a
# MARKER_END hit which no longer fires.
#
# Use --format=gcc for any such count, not the default output: the default ends
# with a "For more information" footer carrying one line per unique code, and
# that line quotes a sample message — so it matches both `grep SC2034` and
# `grep GROUP_` and inflates each tally by one. Measured here 2026-08-14: the
# default format answers 30 and 21 where the real counts are 29 and 20.
